== Light
Usually, a fire will illuminate the surroundings and the atmosphere, so that can be regarded as a point source of light, but not always.  
$ text(#red, f i r e) != "point source of light" $
Some sorts of fire do not shine light on nearby objects, they produce flame and heat only. Such as natural gas fire. Perhaps you can see that phenomenon in your kitchen at night.
$ 2C_2H_6 + 7O_2 = 4C O_2 + 6H_2O $
$ C_3H_8 + 5O_2 = 3C O_2 + 4H_2O $
$ 2C_4H_10 + 13O_2 = 8C O_2 + 10H_2O $
But that's weird. If the fire doesn't produce light, how do you see its flame?
